  "query_preview": "..\n(Monday night through next Saturday)\nIssued at 1125 AM CDT Sun Sep 6 2026\n\nHigh temps creep up beginning Tuesday as the H5 high amplifies to \naround 596 dam squarely over the forecast area. Not until Wednesday \ndoes this high flatten and split into two entities - one taking aim \non the Desert Southwest with the other heading for the Ozarks. \nRegardless, our high temps remain toasty as the H7 high along with\ndrier air at the top of the mixing layer arrives overhead. The \nlatest NBM has warmed and now puts Wednesday's record high of 102 \nat Childress in jeopardy. Meanwhile, Lubbock's record high for \nthis day is 99 which is also within reach. There is also a signal \nfor convective outflows and/or a weak cold front to reach portions\nof the southern TX Panhandle late in the day Wednesday. These \nboundaries along with steady cooling aloft from weak height falls\nsupports 20-40 PoPs from Wednesday evening through Thursday. \n\nEnsemble spread regarding a zonal H5 ridge axis remains a concern\nfor Friday and Saturday as models are struggling with this \nfeature and its impact on any additional PoPs for these two days. \nBy late in the weekend, global models are in decent agreement with\na trough lifting out from the Great Divide while spreading lower \nheights our way. NBM's PoPs capture some of this unsettled pattern\nbeginning Saturday night, before a potentially wetter and cooler \nsignal brews late next weekend and beyond as southwest flow aloft \naligns with a cold front and monsoonal moisture.",
